2. Why Redis (Persistent Cache) is Faster than a Database
RAM-based storage → Redis keeps data in memory (RAM), while databases rely mainly on disk. Accessing RAM is much faster than disk I/O. (Main reason)
Lightweight structures → Redis uses simple key-value, hash, list, set structures instead of complex SQL schemas.
Low latency → Typical Redis operations take sub-milliseconds; DB queries are slower due to parsing, indexing, and disk access.
Efficient design → Single-threaded event loop avoids locks/context switching, unlike multi-threaded databases.
Optimized throughput → Supports pipelining (batch commands in one trip).
Persistence is secondary → Redis writes to disk asynchronously for durability, while databases must enforce ACID rules on every operation, adding overhead.
⚖️ Trade-off
Redis → Speed, caching hot data, simple lookups.
Database → Reliability, durability, transactions, complex queries.

