4. Solution is Introduce Cache
With “cache” I always mean in-memory caches like Memcached or Redis. Please never do file-based caching, it makes cloning and auto-scaling of your servers just a pain. 
why?
--- File cache lives on each app server, so it’s not shared; server failure = cache lost.
Redis lives on dedicated nodes, centralized, and can be replicated and clustered → safer, faster, and shared.
Even if one Redis node dies, replicas or cluster nodes keep cache alive, unlike file-based cache where loss is permanent.

🏷️ Two Caching Patterns----------------------------
1. Cached Database Queries (Old Pattern)
Store query results in cache using a hashed query as the key.
Issues: Hard to expire cached results when underlying data changes.
Problematic for complex queries – updating one piece of data may require deleting many cached queries.
2. Cached Objects (Recommended)
Treat your data as objects (e.g., class instances).
After assembling the object from multiple DB requests, store the full object or dataset in cache.
Advantages:
Easier to invalidate when something changes.
Faster and more logical code flow.
Enables asynchronous processing: worker servers assemble objects; app servers just read cached objects.

------------Asynchronous Processing-----------------
Two Async Paradigms

Async #1 – Pre-compute / Pre-render
Do time-consuming work before user requests it (e.g., pre-render pages into static HTML).
Benefits: Low request time, highly scalable, works well with CDNs (S3/Cloudfront).
Often implemented via scheduled scripts or cronjobs.

Async #2 – On-demand / Job Queue
Handle tasks cannot be pre-computed (e.g., personalized reports).
Workflow:
User triggers a long-running task on the frontend.
Frontend sends job to a queue and immediately responds: “Job in progress.”
Workers process queued jobs.
Once done, frontend is notified and informs the user.
Tools: RabbitMQ, ActiveMQ, Redis lists, or other job queues.
Benefits: Backends are scalable, frontends remain responsive.
Key Takeaways
Always perform time-consuming tasks asynchronously.
Async #1 → Pre-compute predictable content.
Async #2 → Use job queues for heavy or unpredictable tasks.
Improves scalability, performance, and user experience.
